-- An @nlg that belongs to no node is not attached, and says so, whether the
-- rule before it is written as one definition or as clauses. The CONSIDERs a
-- group of clauses is lowered to hold names that the parser made up, and the
-- group's range ran over what followed it; they used to take such an @nlg
-- without a word (refutation of legalese/l4-ide#545's review, round 4). And an
-- @nlg above a group's first clause and a herald on its head collide there, as
-- they do on a rule written as one definition, where neither is attached.
DECLARE Colour IS ONE OF Red, Green

GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE one Red IS 1
DECIDE one Green IS 2
@nlg above the next rule's GIVEN, which no rule has %c%
GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE two c IS 3

GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
@nlg outside, above the first clause %c%
DECIDE three [inside, on the head %c%] Red IS 1
DECIDE three Green IS 2

GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
@nlg outside, above the rule %c%
DECIDE four [inside, on the head %c%] c IS 1

-- An @nlg inside a WHERE of a clause is not written between two clauses, so no
-- later clause takes it: it is the helper's, here as in a rule written as one
-- definition (it used to be reported as not attached).
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE five 0 IS g 1
  WHERE
    @nlg the helper of the first clause %x%
    g x MEANS x + 1
DECIDE five other IS 2

GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE six Red IS 1
DECIDE six Green IS 2
@nlg after the last rule of the file
